# The stall, in a light aeroplane: entering it, recognising it, and getting
# out of it with the least height lost.
#
#   name TEXT...                   what the lesson is called
#   teaches CLASS                  which class of aircraft it is for
#   stage TEXT...                  a stage, in the order flown
#   do TEXT...                     what to do at this stage
#   until PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE     what ends the stage
#   hold PROPERTY LOW HIGH TEXT... a band that must hold for the whole stage
#   need PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE TEXT...  what must be true by the stage's end
#
# with `#` beginning a comment; src/sim/lesson.hpp owns the format.
#
# **The speeds are each aeroplane's own.** `stall` is the published stall
# speed with everything down, which `sim::approach_speeds` works out on the
# way to the reference speed. The four light aircraft stall between about 33
# knots and about 50: a number here would have the Cub stalling at the
# Cessna's speed and teach nothing to either.
#
# **The height is against where the stage began.** A stall is practised at a
# safe height, and what the lesson teaches is how little of it you need to
# give up - not what the altimeter reads.
#
# **This is the clean stall, wings level, power off.** The stall in the
# landing configuration and the stall off a turn are their own exercises and
# are not written yet.
#
# **The height allowed in the recovery is measured.** Stalled from five
# thousand feet and recovered as the wing gives up - control column forward,
# full power - the four light aircraft lost between sixty and two hundred
# feet: the Cub 59, the 172 189, the 182 196, the PA-28 170. Three hundred
# feet is therefore that with a hundred feet of margin. Since the recovery
# became the autopilot's stall recovery (2026-09-26) they lose less: the Cub
# 40, the 172 132, the 182 164, the PA-28 152.
#
# **What it takes to break it, said so the band can be judged.** The 172 left
# twenty-five seconds past the stall loses about three hundred and thirty feet
# and scrapes through; left thirty-five seconds it loses four hundred and
# twenty-eight and is named. (Those figures moved on 2026-09-22 when the
# autopilot stopped snapping its pitch command to the edge of its envelope:
# the same late recovery costs less height now, because the entry is flown
# more gently.) A recovery that late is a long way from a recovery, and a band that
# caught a twelve-second delay would be catching the difference between two
# good recoveries rather than the difference between a good one and a bad
# one.

# **The stall is entered in the landing configuration**, flaps and gear as
# they are for the landing, because `stall` is the stall with everything
# down. Flown clean, the PA-28 lost 321 feet entering it - five times the
# others - and this band was four hundred feet wide on its account alone.
# Entered as its reference speed was measured, all four hold their height to
# within 74 feet: the 172 74, the 182 68, the PA-28 62, the Cub 28. The band
# is those with room.

name Stalls in a light aeroplane
teaches light-aircraft

stage Entering the stall
do Close the throttle and hold the height as the speed comes back
do Keep the wings level and the ball centred: a wing drop is what you are avoiding
do Raise the nose as she slows, and watch the speed
hold position/h-agl-ft start-150 start+100 Hold the height as the speed comes back
until velocities/vc-kts <= stall+6

stage The recovery
do Move the control column forward to unstall the wing: that is the whole recovery
do Full power as the nose comes down, and level the wings
do Ease out of the descent once the speed is back, without stalling her again
need position/h-agl-ft >= start-300 Recover with the least height you can
until velocities/vc-kts >= climb
